# `val t = """a""""` - four closing quotes, and the close is greedy.
#
# Kotlin terminates a raw string at the *last* `"""` in a run, so the content
# is `a"` and the literal spans all eight bytes [8, 16). A first-match reader
# closes at [8, 15) and leaves a stray `"` behind, which is why `roots 1` and
# `mends 0` discriminate here even without the span: the leftover quote has
# nowhere to go. The span pins which of the two readings produced the root.
#
# This is the strongest claim in the tier and the one with no oracle behind
# it - there is no tree-sitter in this checkout, so it is derived from the
# Kotlin grammar's own raw-string rule and the language reference, not
# measured. Read RESULT-1 before trusting it.
roots 1
mends 0
holds string_literal
spans string_literal 8 16
spans string_content 11 13
